--TEST--
`$uri->queryParameter` reads one query parameter by name, and answers `null` for a name that is not there — `rule:core-classes/uri-removable-components`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::queryParameter
// The rule's second level, read half. A URI's components are fixed and few,
// so `with` can name each of them as a bag key; a parameter's name is chosen
// at run time, so it gets a member instead. The answer comes through
// `Core\Uri::parseQuery`'s bracket walk, not a second one.
// The brackets are written percent-encoded because RFC 3986 admits `[` only
// around an IP address in the host, and `Core\Uri::buildQuery` writes them
// the same way.
var $u = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a?x=1&y=two&a%5Bb%5D=c&e=");

echo $u->queryParameter("x") as bytes as string, "\n";
echo $u->queryParameter("y") as bytes as string, "\n";

// A pair written with no value has empty `bytes`, which is not `null` —
// `parseQuery`'s own answer, reached through the same code.
echo "[", $u->queryParameter("e") as bytes as string, "]\n";

// The name is a top-level name and the brackets belong to the value, so `a`
// answers the nested array and `a[b]` names nothing at all.
array<mixed> $nested = $u->queryParameter("a") as array<mixed>;
echo $nested["b"] as bytes as string, "\n";
echo $u->queryParameter("a[b]") == null ? "null" : "found", "\n";

// A name that is not there and a URI with no query at all are one answer.
// A program that must tell those apart asks `query()`, which is `null` for
// the second and `""` for a `?` written with nothing after it.
echo $u->queryParameter("z") == null ? "null" : "found", "\n";
echo Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a")->queryParameter("x") == null ? "null" : "found", "\n";
--EXPECT--
1
two
[]
c
null
null
null
